 not be behind the wheel what's  somewhat difficult with marijuana is  determining what impairment means it can  be difficult and it's tempting to say  well let's just draw this hard line but  if you're putting people in a situation  where they smoked a joint legally three  weeks ago but they're subject to a DUI  every time they get behind the car  during those three weeks that's not fair  that's not legal and and my big concern  as I said familiarity with marijuana is  what's moving this forward it makes it  hard for that level of society that  people really spec respect to admit that  they use marijuana because there's all  these negative consequences waiting for  you that might not be the criminal  justice system well in a DUI it is but  so you know there's ways to look at  active amounts of THC in the bloodstream  as opposed to just the presence of THC I  know there's a lot of efforts to work  that out I'm sure how it's coming  together you can you can talk more about  that yeah what's happening Colorado with  it sure so in Washington's initiative  first it was in the initiative and so it  said there's a 5 nanograms  of THC per milliliter of blood  immediately in there in amendment 64 in  Colorado that was not included but it  did state clearly that you're not  allowed to drive under the influence  which is already illegal it's still  illegal that said over the last three  years legislature has tried to pass a  DUI d driving to the influence of drugs  bill and it has failed because of the  fact that it would be through a blood  test and there's a again civil liberty  concerns there and law enforcement has  concerns about their liability and  whether or not they can be taking  roadside blood tests what they need to  take people into the sheriff's office or  police department to do the tests and  things like that I think the answer the  long-term answer for this is going to be  entrepreneurship I think it's going to  be somebody invents a non-invasive  saliva test