ions while  helicopters flew over your house but now  we're going to legalize it so we have no  you know fu I'm not with that but also  people have to recognize what keeps the  prices up and that's what they're  worried about is people getting busted  that's the prohibition tax and so in one  way you have a small group of people who  were very vocal and had a news story  that pot heads against legalization  that's a story even if it's ten people  with no support out ranting in the  streets you know so I think it got  overblown in a lot of ways I think well  I in my position for the last 10 years  I've dealt with nothing but people who  grow marijuana or are very tied into  this culture and the people i have met  who are not a hundred percent supportive  of marijuana legalization is a very very  small number and it was  disproportionately covered in the media  great well that's good to hear in lots  of ways and harris what do you think of  friends and foes in colorado well one of  the most interesting aspects of the  implementation in colorado is and this  may offend libertarian sentiments but  the regulators and bureaucrats have been  some of the best advocates for a  functional competitive system and that's  the folks of the Department of Revenue  and they're doing a great job in the  public hearing process throughout the  task force meetings basically laying out  what they think is important and why  they think a functional industry is the  best for them and I think it's it's good  basically their incentives align with a  productive industry that generates a lot  of money that pays them application fees  and pays tax revenue to them to you know  promote and allow them to continue to do  their activity they don't want a  fledgling tiny  grey market pseudo a legal business that  they can't make any revenue off of and  so because of that they've been really  pushing back on some of the bad ideas  that would limit the ability of  businesses to operate and grow and then  they've been supporting some of the good  ones and so that I think as one of the  most interesting stories and to their  credit the medical marijuana Enforcement  Division I don't dish out praise a lot  for public agencies so I really want to  be on the record I think they're doing a  great job and so that's one of the big  ones I think another friend to think  about is going to be the press corps the  Denver press corps folks with The  Associated Press the Denver Post  westward which is an alternative you  know locally circulated paper they're  doing a pretty good job I think handling  it responsibility in treating this like  a policy issue which is what it is and I  think that combination of things is  allowing Colorado to move forward and  I'm very optimistic things are going  well as far as treating this like a  serious issue which it is and what about  the existing medical marijuana  dispensary owners in Colorado are they  getting behind it or what what's their  role going forward and do you should  talk a little bit maybe get some  background about who gets to who gets  the priority when they get when it goes  retailer eventually sure so that's  definitely a huge aspect of it so in  amendment 64 it's really clear that the  medical and recreational industries are  separate but it's unavoidable that the  existing medical industry growers  retailers MIPP so the ingestible product  companies and testing companies and  things like that have a seat at the  table they're the ones that deal with  the Department of Revenue right now and  they've like they was saying they've  risked a lot to operate under violation  of federal law and so they deserve  credit for that and the amendment 64 the  language does say that they get a  priority or preference when applying for  recreational licenses as well and so  there's a few structural things where  they deserve you know it's important to  see where they're special or with it how  their unique but there are aspects of  the