ington marijuana legalization  outperformed the president so you know  on what basis are you going to come in  and do these things and and try you know  I think that the federal government I  you know I will say I'm pro-cannabis I  understand also some people say well  let's limit the harms of the these laws  and they're my allies but I I think it's  a good thing not for every person and  not all the time but I think society  will be  better place but I recognize the federal  government has a responsibility to stop  interstate traffic at this point no one  in Oklahoma said we want to legalize  marijuana yet and well some people did  but not enough that's reasonable but to  come into a state or to say I cannot go  into Colorado and have the same rights  as a Colorado right yeah that to me is  ridiculous and and then encouraged by  the non-response of the federal  government at this point great are you  equally optimistic Harris I am I will  say maybe the federal government to be a  frenemy right so they're friendly but  maybe also enemies I'll say one quick  thing the only clear thing that I've  heard that would say that would  definitely cause the federal government  to get involved would be if either  Colorado or Washington were to set up  state-run marijuana stores like you have  in a lot of states where you have  state-run liquor or alcohol retail  stores or wholesaling and dr. Sam Kamin  who's a law professor at the University  of Denver was also on the larger task  force he's basically said that's the  only thing that he can think of from a  legal perspective that would guarantee  the federal government would come in  because states would mean it would be  like if states were gun trafficking or I  mean it's almost impossible some people  have made the argument that they should  do that and I'm skeptical of the  motivations for that argument it seems  like that would be trying to set these  legalizing states up to fail and it  would be almost necessitating the  